proofs.proof_tex:
Fix \(j\in\mathcal J\).  By the definitions of the inverse-feature bridges and the conditional measurement laws, and using the inverses supplied by the surrogate- and proxy-rank assumptions,
\[
\begin{aligned}
 E_P\{a_j(A,X)\mid J=\ell,X\}
 &=e_j^{\mathsf T}M_A(X)^{-1}M_A(X)e_\ell
 =\mathbf 1\{j=\ell\},\\
 E_P\{z_j(Z,X)\mid J=\ell,X\}
 &=e_j^{\mathsf T}M_Z(X)^{-1}M_Z(X)e_\ell
 =\mathbf 1\{j=\ell\}. \tag{1}
\end{aligned}
\]
By three-view conditional independence, conditional on \((J=\ell,X)\) the three factors \(a_j(A,X)\), \(z_j(Z,X)\), and \(Y-m_j(X)\) are independent.  Consequently,
\[
 E_P(U_j\mid J=\ell,X)
 =m_j(X)+\frac{\mathbf 1\{j=\ell\}}{\pi_j(X)}
 E_P\{Y-m_j(X)\mid J=j,X\}=m_j(X). \tag{2}
\]
The last conditional expectation is zero by the definition of \(m_j\).  Thus \(E_P(U_j)=E_P\{m_j(X)\}=\psi_j\), and \(\phi_j=U_j-\psi_j\) is centered.

Consider an observed DQM path.  Differentiating its identified product factorization gives factorwise centered derivative scores; the preliminary density scores are integrable because the factors are probability laws, and \(\phi_j\) is bounded by the calculation below.  Theorem \(\mathrm{differential\text{-}score\text{-}lifting}\) supplies an equivalent square-integrable additive representative of the same observed score.  For either representation, repeated conditioning, (1), and factorwise centering give
\[
\begin{aligned}
 E_P(\phi_js_X)&=E_P[\{m_j(X)-\psi_j\}s_X(X)],\\
 E_P(\phi_js_\pi)&=0,\\
 E_P(\phi_js_Y)&=E_P\!\left[
 \frac{\mathbf 1\{J=j\}}{\pi_j(X)}
 \{Y-m_j(X)\}s_Y(Y,J,X)\right],\\
 E_P(\phi_js_A)&=E_P(\phi_js_Z)=0. \tag{3}
\end{aligned}
\]
For example, the propensity line follows from (2) and \(E_P(s_\pi\mid X)=0\).  For the \(A\)-score line, the term \(m_j(X)-\psi_j\) vanishes against \(E_P(s_A\mid J,X)=0\); in the residual term, \(\ell=j\) gives \(E_P(Y-m_j\mid J=j,X)=0\), whereas \(\ell\ne j\) gives \(E_P(z_j\mid J=\ell,X)=0\).  The \(Z\)-score line is symmetric.  For the outcome line, both bridges average to \(\mathbf 1\{J=j\}\) by (1).

The first line of (3) is the derivative of the outer expectation over \(P_X\).  The outcome contribution satisfies, by latent positivity and \(P(J=j\mid X)=\pi_j(X)\),
\[
 E_P\!\left[
 \frac{\mathbf 1\{J=j\}}{\pi_j(X)}
 \{Y-m_j(X)\}s_Y\,\middle|\,X\right]
 =E_P[\{Y-m_j(X)\}s_Y\mid J=j,X], \tag{4}
\]
which is the derivative of the class-\(j\) conditional mean.  The target is unchanged by propensity and measurement-factor perturbations, agreeing with the zero lines of (3).  Since \(\phi_j\) is \(O\)-measurable, conditional-expectation duality gives
\[
 E_P\!\left[\phi_jE_P\{s_X+s_\pi+s_Y+s_A+s_Z\mid O\}\right]
 =E_P\{\phi_j(s_X+s_\pi+s_Y+s_A+s_Z)\}. \tag{5}
\]
Equations (3)--(5) therefore equal the pathwise derivative of \(\psi_j\) for every observed DQM path, proving the influence-function equation.

The bounded-outcome assumption gives \(|m_j(X)|\leq L\).  Latent positivity gives \(\pi_j^{-1}\leq\varepsilon^{-1}\), and the two measurement-rank assumptions give
\(\|M_A^{-1}\|_{\mathrm{op}}\vee\|M_Z^{-1}\|_{\mathrm{op}}\leq\varepsilon^{-1}\).  Since the category vectors have unit norm, \(\phi_j\) is bounded by a constant depending only on the regular stratum, and hence \(\phi_j\in L^2_0(P)\).

Finally, the cited Bian--Zhou--Cui lemma supplies the categorical inverse-feature and pair-product construction.  In its categorical-action formula, take the state to be \(X\), set the discount to zero, use the same initial and observed state law \(P_X\), let the target policy put unit mass on class \(j\), take behavior propensity \(\pi_j(X)\), reward \(Y\), and inverse features \(a_j\) and \(z_j\).  The resulting displayed score is exactly \(U_j-\psi_j\).  This proves both the stated specialization and its validity here.

Fix \((y,x)\) outside the null sets in the assumptions and represent an observed-cell function by \(k=(k_{az})\in\mathbb R^{K^2}\).  Conditional three-view independence gives
\[
 E_P\{k(O)\mid Y=y,J=j,X=x\}
 =\sum_{a,z}M_A(x)_{aj}M_Z(x)_{zj}k_{az}
 =B_{\cdot j}(x)^{\mathsf T}k. \tag{27}
\]
Thus the outcome-score restrictions from Theorem 2 are exactly \(B^{\mathsf T}k=0\).  Lemma 3A proves that \(N_B\) is idempotent, \(D\)-self-adjoint, and has range \(\ker(B^{\mathsf T})\); hence it is the pointwise \(D\)-orthogonal projector onto the outcome restrictions.  Moreover \(R\mathbf 1_K=\mathbf 1_{K^2}\), because every row of the posterior matrix sums to one.  Lemma 3A then gives \(N_B\mathbf 1_{K^2}=0\), so \(N_Bf_0\) has conditional mean zero given \((Y,X)\).

For a coordinate \(r=(j,a)\), Bayes' identity and iterated expectation give
\[
\begin{aligned}
 E_P\{kR_{\cdot j}\mathbf 1(A=a)\mid X\}
 &=E_P\{k\mathbf 1(J=j)\mathbf 1(A=a)\mid X\}\\
 &=\pi_j(X)E_P\{k\mathbf 1(A=a)\mid J=j,X\}. \tag{28}
\end{aligned}
\]
The analogous identity holds with \(Z=z\).  Since \(\pi_j\geq\varepsilon\), the remaining measurement restrictions in Theorem 2 are exactly \(E_P(kc_r\mid X)=0\) for all \(r\).  If \(k=N_Bk\), conditional \(D\)-self-adjointness yields
\[
 E_P(kc_r\mid X)=E_P\{k(N_Bc_r)\mid X\}=E_P(kh_r\mid X). \tag{29}
\]

If \(v\in\ker G(X)\), then
\[
 0=v^{\mathsf T}G(X)v=E_P\{(v^{\mathsf T}h)^2\mid X\}, \tag{30}
\]
so \(v^{\mathsf T}h=0\) conditionally and \(v^{\mathsf T}b_{f_0}=0\).  Hence \(b_{f_0}\perp\ker G=\operatorname{range}(G)^\perp\), and therefore \(b_{f_0}\in\operatorname{range}(G)\).  It follows that
\[
 E_P\{h(Qf_0)\mid X\}
 =b_{f_0}-GG^+b_{f_0}=0. \tag{31}
\]
Every coordinate of \(h=N_Bc\) is in \(\operatorname{range}(N_B)\), so \(Qf_0\) remains in that range.  Equations (27)--(31) and Theorem 2 imply \(Qf_0\in\mathcal T^\perp\).

For the projection property, take \(k\in\mathcal T^\perp\).  Pointwise \(D\)-orthogonality gives
\[
 E_P[((I-N_B)f_0)k\mid Y,X]=0. \tag{32}
\]
Equation (29) gives \(E_P(hk\mid X)=0\), whence
\[
 E_P\{h^{\mathsf T}G^+b_{f_0}\,k\}
 =E_P[(G^+b_{f_0})^{\mathsf T}E_P(hk\mid X)]=0. \tag{33}
\]
Thus \(E_P\{(f_0-Qf_0)k\}=0\).  The space characterized by the three conditional-zero restrictions is an intersection of kernels of continuous conditional-expectation maps and is closed.  Therefore \(Q\) is the \(L^2(P)\)-orthogonal projection onto \(\mathcal T^\perp\).  Cell positivity bounds conversions between the conditional \(D\)-norm and Euclidean cell norm; the posterior and projection Gram gaps bound the displayed inverses; and constant rank makes the pseudoinverse Borel measurable by the same finite-minor chart argument used in Lemma 2A.  Conditional Cauchy--Schwarz then proves that all terms are in \(L^2(P)\), completing the assertion.

Fix \(c_j\in\mathcal C_{\mathrm{adj}}\) and abbreviate \(v=v_j\), \(b=b_v\), and
\[
 e_0=E_P\{(N_Bv)^2\mid X\}. \tag{37}
\]
The augmented matrix is the conditional Gram matrix of \((h^{\mathsf T},N_Bv)^{\mathsf T}\), so adjoining its last coordinate increases rank by at most one.  If \(a\in\ker G\), then
\(E_P\{(a^{\mathsf T}h)^2\mid X\}=0\), hence \(a^{\mathsf T}h=0\) conditionally and \(a^{\mathsf T}b=0\).  Therefore \(b\in\operatorname{range}(G)\).  Multiplication on the left and right by the invertible block-triangular matrices implementing subtraction of \(G^+b\) from the last coordinate gives
\[
 \operatorname{rank}(G_j^{\mathrm{aug}})
 =\operatorname{rank}(G)
 +\mathbf 1\{e_0-b^{\mathsf T}G^+b>0\}. \tag{38}
\]
The generalized Schur residual is
\[
\begin{aligned}
 e_0-b^{\mathsf T}G^+b
 &=E_P\!\left[\{N_Bv-h^{\mathsf T}G^+b\}^2\mid X\right]\\
 &=E_P\{(c_j^{\mathsf T}Q\phi)^2\mid X\}
 =\Lambda_j(X)\geq0. \tag{39}
\end{aligned}
\]
Here the first equality uses \(GG^+b=b\), and the second uses linearity of \(Q\) and the definition of the cell vector \(v_j\).  Equations (38)--(39) prove equivalence of the positive-probability rank increase, \(P_X\{\Lambda_j>0\}>0\), and \(c_j^{\mathsf T}Q\phi\ne0\) in \(L^2(P)\).

Since \(Q\) is an orthogonal projection, \(Q\phi\) and \((I-Q)\phi=\phi^{\mathrm{eff}}\) are orthogonal coordinatewise.  Therefore, for every \(c\in\mathbb R^K\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
 c^{\mathsf T}(V_{\mathrm{raw}}-V_{\mathrm{eff}})c
 &=E_P\{(c^{\mathsf T}\phi)^2-(c^{\mathsf T}(I-Q)\phi)^2\}\\
 &=E_P\{(c^{\mathsf T}Q\phi)^2\}. \tag{40}
\end{aligned}
\]
For \(c=c_j\), iterating (39) over \(X\) gives the stated equality with \(E_P\{\Lambda_j(X)\}\), and it is strictly positive exactly under the observable rank-increase certificate.
